UPDATE(8)            UNIX Programmer's Manual            UPDATE(8)



NAME
     update - periodically update the super block

SYNOPSIS
     update

DESCRIPTION
     Update is a program that executes the sync(2) primitive every
     30 seconds.  This insures that the file system is fairly up to
     date in case of a crash.  This command should not be executed
     directly, but should be executed out of the initialization
     shell command file, /etc/rc, which backgrounds it.

     Nothing else on a running machine calls sync(2).  A dirty
     buffer reaches the disk when something needs the buffer back,
     and a super block when its allocation batch fills; neither is
     a clock.  The boot allocates a block for /etc/utmp and fills
     no batch, so without update a machine powered off at the shell
     prompt leaves a super block that lists a block an inode owns,
     which fsck(8) reports as "on the FREE LIST and also owned by".

FILES
     /etc/rc                starts it

SEE ALSO
     sync(1), sync(2), fsck(8), init(8), rc(8)

BUGS
     With update running, if the machine is powered off just as the
     sync is executed, a file system can still be damaged.  fsck(8)
     is the other half of the arrangement: update shrinks the
     window, fsck cleans up what the window still loses.



